<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="reed-trigonometry">
  <title>Trigonometry</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>A point traveling around a circle creates a repeating wave when we record
    one of its coordinates. This connects the geometry of the unit circle to the
    family of functions</p>
    <p><md>f(x)=A\cos(wx+T).</md></p>
    <p>By the end of this lesson, you will be able to read sine and cosine from
    the unit circle, explain why <m>\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1</m>, and use
    <m>A</m>, <m>w</m>, and <m>T</m> to describe and sketch a cosine wave.
    You need the Pythagorean theorem, coordinates, and basic algebra; no previous
    trigonometry lesson is required.</p>
    <p>Work with pencil and paper as you read. The interactive exercises are
    selected from the Math Trailhead's existing trigonometry worksheets.
    Enter exact answers such as <c>pi/3</c> and <c>sqrt(3)/2</c> unless a problem
    requests decimals. Use radians unless a degree symbol appears.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="reed-trig-circle">
    <title>The unit circle: angles become coordinates</title>
    <p>The <term>unit circle</term> has center <m>(0,0)</m> and radius
    <m>1</m>. Start at <m>(1,0)</m> and turn through an angle <m>\theta</m>.
    Positive angles turn counterclockwise; negative angles turn clockwise.
    The angle determines a point on the circle.</p>
    <p>An angle in <term>radians</term> is the signed distance traveled along a
    circle divided by its radius. On the unit circle, the size of the angle
    equals the arc length. One full turn is <m>2\pi</m> radians because the
    circumference is <m>2\pi</m>. Therefore</p>
    <p><md>360^\circ=2\pi\text{ radians},\qquad
    180^\circ=\pi\text{ radians},\qquad
    90^\circ=\frac{\pi}{2}\text{ radians}.</md></p>
    <p>To convert degrees to radians, multiply by <m>\pi/180</m>; to convert
    radians to degrees, multiply by <m>180/\pi</m>. For example,
    <m>120^\circ=2\pi/3</m> radians and <m>-\pi/4=-45^\circ</m>.</p>
    <p>We define <term>cosine</term> to be the horizontal coordinate and
    <term>sine</term> to be the vertical coordinate:</p>
    <p><md>Q=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta).</md></p>
    <figure xml:id="reed-trig-circle-figure">
      <caption>The radius has length 1. Its horizontal and vertical components
      are cosine and sine.</caption>
      <image source="reed-algebra-lessons/unit-circle.svg" width="85%">
        <shortdescription>A unit circle with a radius to a point in the first quadrant.
        The horizontal leg is labeled cosine theta, the vertical leg sine theta,
        and the radius 1. The four axis intercepts are labeled with coordinates
        and angles 0, pi over 2, pi, and 3 pi over 2.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>In the first quadrant, this agrees with right-triangle trigonometry:
    cosine is adjacent side divided by hypotenuse and sine is opposite side
    divided by hypotenuse. Here the hypotenuse is <m>1</m>. The coordinate
    definition continues to work in every quadrant, including points on the axes.</p>
    <table>
      <title>Five landmarks in one turn</title>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes"><cell><m>\theta</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>\pi</m></cell><cell><m>3\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>2\pi</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\cos\theta</m></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\sin\theta</m></cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>Signs come from coordinates: in quadrant I both are positive; in II
    cosine is negative and sine positive; in III both are negative; in IV
    cosine is positive and sine negative. Also, each coordinate lies between
    <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>.</p>
    <p>You can reconstruct the familiar exact values. At <m>\pi/4</m>, the
    triangle's legs have the same length <m>a</m>, so
    <m>2a^2=1</m> gives <m>a=\sqrt{2}/2</m>. Bisect an equilateral triangle
    of side <m>1</m>: the short leg is <m>1/2</m>, the hypotenuse is
    <m>1</m>, and the long leg is <m>\sqrt{3}/2</m>. This gives the
    <m>\pi/6</m> and <m>\pi/3</m> values.</p>
    <table>
      <title>First-quadrant values to reconstruct</title>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes"><cell>Angle</cell><cell><m>\cos\theta</m></cell><cell><m>\sin\theta</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\pi/6=30^\circ</m></cell><cell><m>\sqrt{3}/2</m></cell><cell><m>1/2</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\pi/4=45^\circ</m></cell><cell><m>\sqrt{2}/2</m></cell><cell><m>\sqrt{2}/2</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\pi/3=60^\circ</m></cell><cell><m>1/2</m></cell><cell><m>\sqrt{3}/2</m></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>A <term>reference angle</term> is the acute angle from a point's radius
    to the horizontal axis. Reflect a first-quadrant point to obtain other
    quadrants, keeping coordinate magnitudes and changing their signs.
    For example, <m>5\pi/6</m> is in quadrant II with reference angle
    <m>\pi/6</m>, so its point is <m>(-\sqrt{3}/2,1/2)</m>.</p>
    <p>Adding or subtracting <m>2\pi</m> makes one extra full turn and returns
    to the same point. Thus <m>-\pi/6</m> and <m>11\pi/6</m> have the same
    sine and cosine. This is why both functions repeat every <m>2\pi</m>.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-degrees">
      <title>Convert degrees to radians</title>
      <!-- Reused from degrees_radians.ptx. -->
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/AngleMeasure-Radians/convert-degrees2rads-med.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-radians">
      <title>Convert radians to degrees</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/AngleMeasure-Radians/convert-rads2degrees-med.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-quadrants">
      <title>Use coordinate signs to identify quadrants</title>
      <!-- Reused from evaluating_trig_functions.ptx. -->
      <webwork source="Library/Rochester/setTrig02FunctionsUnitCircle/srw5_2_47.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-cosine-values">
      <title>Read exact cosine values</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/UnitCircle/unit-circle-cosine.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-sine-values">
      <title>Read exact sine values</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/UnitCircle/unit-circle-sine.pg"/>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="reed-trig-identity">
    <title>One circle, one identity</title>
    <p>Every point <m>(u,v)</m> on the unit circle is one unit from the
    origin. The Pythagorean theorem says <m>u^2+v^2=1</m>. Substitute
    <m>u=\cos\theta</m> and <m>v=\sin\theta</m>:</p>
    <p><md>\boxed{\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta=1.}</md></p>
    <p>That is the whole derivation. The notation <m>\sin^2\theta</m>
    means <m>(\sin\theta)^2</m>, not <m>\sin(\theta^2)</m>. In other
    quadrants, the triangle's side lengths are the absolute values of the
    coordinates. Squaring removes the signs, so the same identity holds for
    every angle, including the axis points.</p>
    <example>
      <title>Recover a missing coordinate</title>
      <statement><p>If <m>\cos\theta=-3/5</m> and <m>\theta</m> is in
      quadrant II, find <m>\sin\theta</m>.</p></statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The identity gives</p>
        <p><md>\sin^2\theta=1-\frac{9}{25}=\frac{16}{25}.</md></p>
        <p>So <m>\sin\theta=\pm4/5</m>. Quadrant II has positive vertical
        coordinate, hence <m>\sin\theta=4/5</m>. The identity determines
        the magnitude; the quadrant determines the sign.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-identity-check">
      <title>Explain the identity</title>
      <statement><p>Draw a unit circle and explain the identity in one sentence.
      Then find <m>\cos\theta</m> when <m>\sin\theta=-5/13</m> and
      <m>\theta</m> is in quadrant III. Finally, simplify
      <m>1-\sin^2\theta</m>.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The squared coordinates of a point on a circle of radius
      <m>1</m> sum to <m>1</m>. Here
      <m>\cos^2\theta=1-25/169=144/169</m>; quadrant III makes cosine
      negative, so <m>\cos\theta=-12/13</m>. For every angle,
      <m>1-\sin^2\theta=\cos^2\theta</m>.</p></solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="reed-trig-waves">
    <title>From a circle to a wave</title>
    <p>Now let the angle vary and graph its horizontal coordinate against the
    angle. This produces <m>y=\cos x</m>. As the point goes around the circle,
    its horizontal coordinate follows the pattern
    <m>1,0,-1,0,1</m> at the five landmarks above. Plotting the vertical
    coordinate instead gives <m>y=\sin x</m>, with pattern
    <m>0,1,0,-1,0</m>.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Cosine and sine record two coordinates of the same circular
      motion. Both repeat after one full turn.</caption>
      <image source="reed-algebra-lessons/sine-cosine.svg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Graphs of cosine and sine from zero to two pi with values
        marked at every quarter turn. Cosine starts at one; sine starts at zero
        and rises. Both return to their initial values after two pi.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>The <term>midline</term> is the line halfway between the maximum and
    minimum; here it is <m>y=0</m>. The <term>amplitude</term> is the distance
    from the midline to a peak; here it is <m>1</m>. The <term>period</term>
    is the length of the shortest positive horizontal shift that repeats the
    entire graph; here it is <m>2\pi</m>. A maximum and the next minimum are
    only half a period apart.</p>
    <p>Sine has the same shape as cosine, shifted right by a quarter turn:</p>
    <p><md>\sin x=\cos\left(x-\frac{\pi}{2}\right).</md></p>
    <p>To see this on the circle, rotate <m>(u,v)</m> clockwise by a quarter
    turn. Its coordinates become <m>(v,-u)</m>, so the new horizontal
    coordinate is the old vertical coordinate. This also lets us describe
    sine waves using the cosine family.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-circle-rotations">
      <title>Practice turns and reflections on the circle</title>
      <!-- Reused from sine_cosine.ptx. -->
      <webwork source="Library/LoyolaChicago/Precalc/Chap6Sec3/Q40.pg"/>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="reed-trig-parameters">
    <title>What A, w, and T do</title>
    <p>In <m>f(x)=A\cos(wx+T)</m>, call the angle inside cosine
    <m>\theta=wx+T</m>. Starting with an input <m>x</m>, first compute this
    angle, then read its horizontal coordinate on the unit circle, then
    multiply that coordinate by <m>A</m>. The parameter <m>w</m> is often
    written as the Greek letter <m>\omega</m>. We use <m>P</m> for the
    period: <m>T</m> here is an angle, not the period.</p>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>A controls vertical size and reflection</title>
      <p>Multiplying by <m>A</m> multiplies every height by <m>A</m>. Thus
      <m>3\cos x</m> ranges from <m>-3</m> to <m>3</m>, and
      <m>\tfrac12\cos x</m> ranges from <m>-1/2</m> to <m>1/2</m>.
      A negative <m>A</m> also reflects the graph across the horizontal axis:
      <m>-3\cos x</m> begins at <m>-3</m> rather than <m>3</m>.
      Amplitude is a distance, so it is <m>|A|</m>, never negative.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-amplitude-read">
      <title>Read amplitude from a graph</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/GraphingSineCosine/identify-amplitude.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-amplitude-match">
      <title>Match amplitudes and reflections</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/Precalculus/setTrigonometry_-_Graphing_Amplitude/matching-amplitudes.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>w controls how quickly the angle changes</title>
      <p>Increasing <m>x</m> by <m>\Delta x</m> changes the angle by
      <m>w\Delta x</m>. A complete turn requires an angular change of size
      <m>2\pi</m>. For <m>A\ne0</m> and <m>w\ne0</m>,</p>
      <p><md>|w|P=2\pi,\qquad \boxed{P=\frac{2\pi}{|w|}.}</md></p>
      <p>For example, <m>\cos(2x)</m> makes a full turn while <m>x</m>
      increases by only <m>\pi</m>; its graph is horizontally compressed.
      The graph of <m>\cos(x/2)</m> needs <m>4\pi</m> units for a turn and
      is horizontally stretched. Larger <m>|w|</m> means a shorter period.</p>
      <p>If <m>w&lt;0</m>, the angle decreases as <m>x</m> increases, so the
      point travels clockwise. The period still uses <m>|w|</m>. Reflecting
      the circle across the horizontal axis shows
      <m>\cos(-\theta)=\cos\theta</m>, so
      <m>\cos(-2x)=\cos(2x)</m>. With a nonzero phase angle, keep track of
      that sign too: <m>\cos(-2x+T)=\cos(2x-T)</m>.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-period-match">
      <title>Match graphs by their periods</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/Precalculus/setTrigonometry_-_Graphing_Period/matching-period.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>T controls the starting angle</title>
      <p>At <m>x=0</m>, the angle is <m>T</m>, so <m>f(0)=A\cos T</m>.
      To locate the horizontal shift, factor out <m>w</m>:</p>
      <p><md>w x+T=w\left(x+\frac{T}{w}\right)=w(x-h),
      \qquad \boxed{h=-\frac{T}{w}.}</md></p>
      <p>The graph of <m>A\cos(w(x-h))</m> is the graph of
      <m>A\cos(wx)</m> shifted right by <m>h</m> if <m>h&gt;0</m>, or left
      by <m>|h|</m> if <m>h&lt;0</m>. Equivalently, solve <m>w x+T=0</m>
      to find where the original angle-zero point has moved.</p>
      <p>For example, <m>\cos(2x+\pi/2)=\cos(2(x+\pi/4))</m>
      shifts <m>\cos(2x)</m> left by <m>\pi/4</m>, not <m>\pi/2</m>.
      The plus sign describes a head start around the circle. The graph reaches
      a given angle earlier, which explains the shift to the left when
      <m>w&gt;0</m>.</p>
      <p>Changing <m>T</m> does not change amplitude or period. Adding
      <m>2\pi k</m> to <m>T</m>, for any integer <m>k</m>, gives the same
      function; horizontal shifts that differ by whole periods are equivalent.
      If you change <m>w</m> while keeping <m>T</m> fixed, both the period
      and the shift <m>-T/w</m> generally change.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <table>
      <title>The nonconstant family at a glance</title>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes"><cell>Feature</cell><cell><m>A\cos(wx+T)</m>, with <m>A,w\ne0</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Amplitude</cell><cell><m>|A|</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Midline and range</cell><cell><m>y=0</m>; <m>[-|A|,|A|]</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Period</cell><cell><m>P=2\pi/|w|</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Horizontal shift</cell><cell><m>h=-T/w</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Value at zero</cell><cell><m>A\cos T</m></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>Two exceptions deserve their own check: if <m>A=0</m>, then
    <m>f(x)=0</m>; if <m>w=0</m>, then <m>f(x)=A\cos T</m> is constant.
    Constant functions have no smallest positive period. In particular, do not
    divide by <m>w</m> when <m>w=0</m>.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-shift-check">
      <title>Read the inside carefully</title>
      <statement><p>For <m>g(x)=-2\cos(3x-\pi)</m>, find the amplitude,
      period, horizontal shift, and <m>g(0)</m>. Explain why its amplitude is
      not negative and its shift is not <m>\pi</m>.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The amplitude is <m>2</m>, the period is <m>2\pi/3</m>,
      and <m>3x-\pi=3(x-\pi/3)</m> gives a shift right by
      <m>\pi/3</m>. Also <m>g(0)=-2\cos(-\pi)=2</m>. The minus sign
      reflects the graph; amplitude is a positive distance. The phase angle
      must be divided by the coefficient of <m>x</m> to find the shift.</p></solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-phase-graphs">
      <title>Write equations for shifted cosine graphs</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/Precalculus/setTrigonometry_-_Graphing_Phase_Shift/short-answer-phaseshift-cosine.pg"/>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="reed-trig-put-together">
    <title>Build a graph, then read an equation from a graph</title>
    <p>For <m>w&gt;0</m>, the five quarter-turn landmarks occur at
    <m>x=h,h+P/4,h+P/2,h+3P/4,h+P</m>. Their heights are
    <m>A,0,-A,0,A</m>. This works even when <m>A&lt;0</m>: the first point
    is then a minimum. Join the landmarks with a smooth wave and repeat.</p>
    <example xml:id="reed-trig-worked-wave">
      <title>Sketch <m>f(x)=3\cos(2x+\pi/2)</m></title>
      <statement><p>Find its amplitude, period, and shift, and sketch one cycle.</p></statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Here <m>A=3</m>, <m>w=2</m>, and <m>T=\pi/2</m>. The
        amplitude is <m>3</m>, <m>P=\pi</m>, and <m>h=-\pi/4</m>.
        Each quarter period has length <m>\pi/4</m>.</p>
        <table>
          <title>Follow the angle inside cosine</title>
          <tabular halign="center">
            <row header="yes"><cell><m>x</m></cell><cell><m>-\pi/4</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\pi/4</m></cell><cell><m>\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>3\pi/4</m></cell></row>
            <row><cell><m>2x+\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>\pi</m></cell><cell><m>3\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>2\pi</m></cell></row>
            <row><cell><m>f(x)</m></cell><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>-3</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>3</m></cell></row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>The graph starts this cycle at a maximum, falls through the midline,
        reaches a minimum, and returns to the maximum. As a check,
        <m>f(0)=3\cos(\pi/2)=0</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <figure>
      <caption>One cycle of <m>3\cos(2x+\pi/2)</m>. The peak has moved
      left to <m>-\pi/4</m>, and consecutive peaks are <m>\pi</m> apart.</caption>
      <image source="reed-algebra-lessons/transformed-cosine.svg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>A cosine wave through the five points negative pi over four,
        three; zero, zero; pi over four, negative three; pi over two, zero; and
        three pi over four, three. Amplitude three and period pi are marked.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <example>
      <title>Choose parameters from a graph</title>
      <statement><p>A cosine-shaped wave has maximum <m>2</m>, minimum
      <m>-2</m>, and consecutive maxima at <m>x=1</m> and <m>x=5</m>.
      Find an equation.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The midline is <m>0</m> and the amplitude is
      <m>(2-(-2))/2=2</m>. Choose <m>A=2</m>. The period is
      <m>5-1=4</m>, so choose <m>w=2\pi/4=\pi/2</m>. Place a peak at
      <m>h=1</m>:</p>
      <p><md>f(x)=2\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}(x-1)\right)
      =2\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}x-\frac{\pi}{2}\right).</md></p>
      <p>Thus one choice is <m>T=-\pi/2</m>. Check that the minimum lies
      halfway between the two maxima, at <m>x=3</m>.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-graph-match">
      <title>Combine amplitude and period</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/GraphingSineCosine/function-graphing.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>A useful extension: move the midline</title>
      <p>Some Trailhead problems include a vertical translation:
      <m>f(x)=D+A\cos(wx+T)</m>. The added <m>D</m> moves the midline
      to <m>y=D</m>; the maximum and minimum become <m>D+|A|</m> and
      <m>D-|A|</m>. Amplitude, period, and horizontal shift stay the same.
      For instance, <m>4+2\cos(3x)</m> has midline <m>4</m>, range
      <m>[2,6]</m>, and period <m>2\pi/3</m>.</p>
      <p>The same transformation rules apply to <m>D+A\sin(w(x-h))</m>.
      For <m>w&gt;0</m>, its quarter-cycle heights, starting at <m>x=h</m>, are
      <m>D,D+A,D,D-A,D</m>. If you convert it to cosine using the
      quarter-turn identity, its phase angle is <m>T=-wh-\pi/2</m>.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-complete-features">
      <title>Extension: describe a shifted sine wave</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CCCS/PreCalculus/08.1/CCD_CCCS_Openstax_AlgTrig_AT-1-001-AS_8_1_22.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-exit-check">
      <title>Check your understanding</title>
      <statement>
        <p><ol>
          <li><p>Sketch one cycle of <m>2\cos(x/2-\pi/2)</m> by listing
          its five quarter-turn points. State its amplitude, period, and shift.</p></li>
          <li><p>A wave with midline <m>0</m> has amplitude <m>4</m>,
          period <m>6</m>, and a maximum at <m>x=-1</m>. Write one
          equation in the form <m>A\cos(wx+T)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>A classmate says <m>\cos^2(2x)+\sin^2(2x)=2</m>
          because the angle doubles. Explain the mistake using the circle.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p><ol>
          <li><p>Amplitude <m>2</m>, period <m>4\pi</m>, shift right
          <m>\pi</m>. The points are <m>(\pi,2)</m>,
          <m>(2\pi,0)</m>, <m>(3\pi,-2)</m>, <m>(4\pi,0)</m>,
          and <m>(5\pi,2)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Choose <m>A=4</m>, <m>w=\pi/3</m>, and
          <m>T=\pi/3</m>, giving <m>4\cos(\pi x/3+\pi/3)</m>.
          At <m>x=-1</m> the angle is zero; six units later it is
          <m>2\pi</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>The angle <m>2x</m> still locates a point on a circle of
          radius <m>1</m>. Its squared coordinates sum to <m>1</m>.
          Changing the angle changes the point, not the radius.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
